.TH UUCP 1C
.SH NAME
uucp, uulog \- unix to unix copy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uucp
[ option ] ...  source-file ...  destination-file
.PP
.B uulog
[ option ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uucp
is used for file transfer between computers running UNIX or
VENIX.
It copies files named by the source-file arguments to the
destination-file argument.
A file name may be a path name on your
machine, or may have the form
.IP
system-name!pathname
.PP
where `system-name' is taken from a list of system names which
.B uucp
knows about.
Shell metacharacters ?*[] appearing in the pathname part
will be expanded on the appropriate system.
Note: when using the C
shell (csh), exclamation points must be preceded by a `\e' to prevent
interpretation.
.PP
Pathnames may be one of
.TP 6
(1)
a full pathname;
.TP
(2)
a pathname preceded by
.RI ~ user ;
where
.I user
is a userid on the
specified system and is replaced by that user's login directory;
.TP
(3)
anything else is prefixed by the current directory.
.PP
If the result is an erroneous pathname for the remote system the copy
will fail.
If the destination-file is a directory, the last part of the source-file
name is used.
.PP
.B uucp
preserves execute permissions across the transmission and gives 0666
read and write permissions (see
.BR chmod (2)).
.PP
The following options are interpreted by
.IR uucp .
.TP 6
.B \-d
Make all necessary directories for the file copy.
.TP
.B \-c
Use the source file when copying out rather than copying the file
to the spool directory.
.TP
.B \-m
Send mail to the requester when the copy is complete.
.PP
.B uulog
maintains a summary log of
.B uucp
and
.BR uux (1)
transactions in the
file `/usr/spool/uucp/LOGFILE' by gathering information from partial
log files named `/usr/spool/uucp/LOG.*.?'.
It removes the partial log
files.
.PP
The options cause
.B uulog
to print logging information:
.TP 8
.BI \-s sys
Print information about work involving system
.IR sys .
.TP
.BI \-u user
Print information about work done for the specified
.IR user .
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 1.8i
/usr/include/indent.h	\- system name
/usr/spool/uucp	\- spool directory
/usr/lib/uucp/*	\- other data and program files
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
uux(1), mail(1)
.br
``UUCP Implementation Description'' in the
.IR "Installation and System Manager's Guide" .
.SH WARNING
The domain of remotely accessible files can (and for obvious security reasons,
usually should) be severely restricted.
You will very likely not be
able to fetch files by pathname; ask a responsible person on the remote
system to send them to you.
For the same reasons you will probably not
be able to send files to arbitrary pathnames.
.SH BUGS
All files received by
.B uucp
will be owned by uucp.
.br
The
.B \-m
option will only work sending files or receiving a single file.
(Receiving multiple files specified by special shell characters ?*[] will not
activate the
.B \-m
option.)
.sp
